[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: r4
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: OSPF inter-area summarization/ABR behavior on r4. Mechanism: r4 is advertising and installing very broad inter-area summaries (/8) in a way that causes a mutual “point-at-each-other” condition with the backbone, so traffic to those summaries loops between ABRs instead of being steered toward the true origin.

Localization and evidence:
- The 1.0.0.0/8 network is actually attached behind r8: on r8 we see “C>* 1.0.0.0/8 is directly connected, eth0”.
- r4 wrongly sends 1.0.0.0/8 toward r3: on r4 “O>* 1.0.0.0/8 … via 34.1.1.1, eth0” (34.1.1.1 is r3).
- r3 does not point to the exit; instead it points away: on r3 “O>* 1.0.0.0/8 … via 23.1.1.1, eth1” (ultimately feeding back toward r4’s side). This produces a forwarding loop across the r3–r4 boundary for 1/8 (and likely other large aggregates).
- On r4’s OSPF process: “This router is an ABR, ABR type is: Alternative Cisco”, indicating nonstandard ABR summarization logic is in use.

Root cause: r4 is operating with “Alternative Cisco” ABR mode and leaking/accepting oversized /8 inter-area summaries such that r3 and r4 each consider the other as best next-hop for 1.0.0.0/8, forming a routing loop.

Corrective fix: On r4, revert to standard ABR behavior (OSPF abr-type standard) or add proper area-range/filtering so only the side owning 1.0.0.0/8 (via r5→r8) advertises it; ensure r4’s FIB prefers the R (to r5) path for 1/8 and does not re-originate that summary back toward r3. Clearing OSPF after the change will break the loop and restore reachability. 
=== END ===